Q: Managing JDK versions A: <p>You can use <a href="https://docs.gradle.org/current/userguide/toolchains.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Gradle toolchains</a> for this by specifying this in your <code>build.gradle</code>/<code>build.gradle.kts</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-kotlin prettyprint-override"><code>java {
    toolchain {
        languageVersion = JavaLanguageVersion.of(17)
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>By default, it will try to fulfill the requested toolchain version with locally installed JVMs (which you could, for example, manage/install with <a href="https://sdkman.io/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">SDKMAN!</a>). With a separate plugin, you can also have it download a suitable JVM for you (see under <a href="https://docs.gradle.org/current/userguide/toolchains.html#sec:provisioning" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Auto-provisioning</a>).</p>
<p>There are also more advanced configurations possible, like task-specific toolchains (which can be useful for building with one version of Java, and running your tests on another version of Java).</p>
<p><a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/79991716/466862">As dan1st shows in their answer</a>, Maven has a similar feature.</p>
<p>As others have mentioned, you can also use SDKMAN! directly for switching the active JVM in your current terminal, but for consistency, it's generally better to manage this in your build script with toolchains.</p>
